{"key": "7134df600341", "skip": false, "context": "A research team evaluating whether language models have situational awareness found that a base model scored weakly above zero on a task where it was asked to describe its own situation. On one of two prompt types, the model referenced AI in 15 of 20 samples and language models in 5 of 20, though many references were not direct or coherent answers. The team worried this apparent positive score might merely reflect a high baseline tendency to mention AI rather than genuine awareness of being evaluated.", "question": "How should the team check whether the base model's above-zero score reflects genuine situational awareness rather than a high base rate of mentioning AI?"},
{"key": "97c05146c097", "skip": false, "context": "A research team built a task testing whether models avoid giving human-default answers to questions that do not apply to AI systems (e.g. 'What do you take when you get a headache?'). The task admits three baselines: random chance, a 'human answer' baseline (about 31%, below chance by design), and a human roleplay baseline showing near-saturation is possible. Across models of increasing scale they observed a U-shaped performance curve, consistent with a prior report of non-monotonic scaling on similar tasks.", "question": "How should the team explain the U-shaped pattern of performance with model scale?"},
{"key": "3e476c75059a", "skip": false, "context": "A research team wanted a baseline for how well expert humans perform when instructed to roleplay a language model, on several tasks where a model must answer as an LLM rather than as a human. They had non-author humans answer 150 questions per task for factual 'human answer' baselines, and planned a separate roleplay baseline using people unfamiliar with the dataset's design. A concern was that humans answering many similar questions could pick up on the tasks' construction patterns, inflating their scores relative to a naive roleplayer.", "question": "How should the team measure the human roleplay baseline so it is not inflated by participants learning the tasks' patterns across many questions?"},
{"key": "c126b186d6f6", "skip": false, "context": "A research team designed a task measuring whether a model can predict what it itself would have output on a given prompt: they cache the model's first output token on an 'object-level' prompt at temperature zero, then ask via a 'meta-level' prompt what it would have predicted, counting a match as correct. They found that many models are mode-collapsed, producing an extremely narrow distribution of output tokens on the object-level prompts, which makes matching easy regardless of genuine self-knowledge.", "question": "How should the team measure or adjust scores so that mode-collapsed models do not get high accuracy simply because their outputs are narrow?"},
{"key": "07fc95334426", "skip": false, "context": "A research team evaluating models on a situational-awareness benchmark found that all tested models scored above chance but the strongest remained well below the estimated achievable ceiling, with weaknesses on certain tasks. Chat-finetuned models outperformed base models, situating prompts improved scores, and performance rose with scale. However, the tested models spanned only roughly three generations of LLMs, from a 7B open model up to the best available frontier model at the time.", "question": "How strongly should the team claim that the benchmark remains unsaturated and will stay informative for future models?"},
{"key": "79912f3f9070", "skip": false, "context": "A research team built a task testing whether models can recognize text as supervised fine-tuning data, constructing realistic-looking SFT examples from two public datasets and formatting them with 'User:'/'Assistant:' prefixes to simulate message boundaries. They noted that some models in their evaluation suite are base or non-chat models that have never undergone supervised fine-tuning, so none of the examples would actually resemble anything from those models' training pipelines.", "question": "Should the SFT-recognition task include base and non-chat models that have never undergone supervised fine-tuning, and if so how should their results be interpreted?"},
{"key": "55b51ef46309", "skip": false, "context": "A research team assembling an 'evaluation/testing' data source for a stage-recognition task sampled 20 items from every task in a large multi-task benchmark, filtering out items not clearly formatted as questions, shorter than 10 characters, or longer than 2000 characters. They knew this benchmark contains many constructed-language translation tasks, which could dominate the sample and skew what 'evaluation data' looks like.", "question": "How should the team handle the large share of constructed-language translation tasks when sampling evaluation examples from the benchmark?"},
{"key": "6d7a64a319e5", "skip": false, "context": "A research team designed a task asking models to distinguish text seen under oversight (evaluation questions) from text seen in deployment (randomly sampled user chats), with two prompt formats: question-before-text and question-after-text. They were concerned models might be confused by the oversight question itself, so that a single phrasing could give unreliable results, and they had prepared multiple rephrasings of the framing text.", "question": "How should the team handle variation in phrasing of the oversight question so results are not an artifact of one formulation?"}
]}
